WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.780
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.080
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.360
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.440
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.420
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:26.210
Merry, Merry Christmas.

9
00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:27.590
Merry, Merry Christmas.

10
00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.650
It's Christmas on The Office again.

11
00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:33.390
Well, someone showed up very festive.

12
00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:34.230
That's right.

13
00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:35.810
It's a Benihana Christmas.

14
00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:40.090
Well, I think Angela Martin would let you know it's a nutcracker Christmas.

15
00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:41.670
Oh, wow.

16
00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:42.810
Oh, wow.

17
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:43.930
You're already going there.

18
00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:44.990
I am, lady.

19
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:45.730
I am here.

20
00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.210
The party planning committee has arrived.

21
00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:50.030
Well, we'll talk about that.

22
00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:59.270
Guys, this is Season 3, Episode 10, written by Jen Salada, directed by Harold Ramis, A Benihana Christmas.

23
00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.790
And this is an hour-long episode.

24
00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:03.510
Yeah, so buckle in.

25
00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.330
It's going to be a super-sized episode of Office Ladies.

26
00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.150
And we have some exciting news.

27
00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:14.910
We have two special guests this week, Rashida Jones and writer Jen Salada.

28
00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:16.310
So much fun.

29
00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.930
And both of these ladies will be joining us for interviews during the episode.

30
00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.010
Well, Jenna, how about giving us a summary?

31
00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:27.210
Okay, it is Christmas at Dunder Mifflin.

32
00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:34.450
Carol breaks up with Michael the day of the Christmas party after Michael photoshops himself

33
00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:36.610
under her family Christmas card.

34
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:44.370
He put his face on her ex-husband's face from a family ski trip and sent it out to people.

35
00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:45.030
Yeah.

36
00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:46.050
Oh, Michael.

37
00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:51.290
Well, Andy suggests he and some of the guys take Michael to Benihana to cheer him up.

38
00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:56.550
Meanwhile, Angela kicks Karen out of the party planning committee.

39
00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:02.190
So Pam and Karen band together and start the committee to plan parties.

40
00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:04.890
We're going to have some dueling holiday parties.

41
00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.650
Oh, she should have had better ideas.

42
00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:10.470
Oh, boy, you you are in it to win it today.

43
00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:11.870
And I came to play.

44
00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:12.710
I came to play.

45
00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:17.870
Well, later, Michael and the boys come back from Benihana with dates just in time for the

46
00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:19.210
two parties to merge.

47
00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:22.970
And we end with everyone singing their hearts out to karaoke.

48
00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:24.850
All right.

49
00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.910
Fast fact number one, Harold Ramis.

50
00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:29.250
Harold Ramis.

51
00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:33.210
I mean, Jenna, my whole childhood were his movies.

52
00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:33.770
Legend.

53
00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:38.630
I was so giddy, nervous, excited to work with him.

54
00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:46.570
I mean, Meatballs, Caddyshack, Ghostbusters, Ghostbusters 2, Groundhog Day for flippin' sake.

55
00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:47.530
Stripes?

56
00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:48.490
Stripes.

57
00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:49.130
Oh, my God.

58
00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:49.430
Yes.

59
00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:50.090
Stripes.

60
00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.670
Oh, also, Angela, he got his start at Chicago's Second City.

61
00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:54.250
Yes.

62
00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:57.010
He's just like so talented.

63
00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.050
He's such a great writer as well.

64
00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.170
You guys, he's a writer, actor, director.

65
00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:06.050
He was just a comic legend, and I was in awe.

66
00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:08.550
Well, you know, he passed away in 2014.

67
00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:12.990
I just can't believe that I got to work with him, frankly.

68
00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:13.730
I know.

69
00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:16.990
Harold Ramis was just a teddy bear of a human.

70
00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:21.370
He just made you feel so comfortable, and he was so fun to talk to.

71
00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.110
And I have some great memories of him.

72
00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:27.990
We did a lot of conference room scenes, obviously, in this episode.

73
00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:30.270
I was in the conference room quite a bit, right?

74
00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:33.110
Because my party versus Karen's and Pam's party.

75
00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:33.450
Yeah.

76
00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:36.870
And Harold was in the conference room with me, and we were just chatting.

77
00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:44.050
And he told this great story about how when kids would come trick-or-treating to his house,

78
00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:45.010
he lived in Illinois.

79
00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:53.090
He would put on the Ghostbusters uniform and answer the door in his Ghostbusters uniform.

80
00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:55.130
Yeah, he did that every year.

81
00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:56.710
I thought that was so delightful.

82
00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:58.190
It was the sweetest story.

83
00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:00.510
All right, let's move in to Fast Fact No.

84
00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:03.390
2, which I'm calling Guest Star Breakdown.

85
00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:03.830
Okay.

86
00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:06.190
We got to break down all of our guest stars.

87
00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.410
The Benihana Waitresses.

88
00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:13.810
The main waitress in the restaurant, Cindy, is played by Brittany Ishibashi,

89
00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:17.190
who you might recognize from Runaways.

90
00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:23.450
She starred as Tina, as well as recurring roles on Hawaii Five-0 and Grace and Frankie.

91
00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:29.710
But here is something very interesting about Brittany and me.

92
00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:30.750
What?

93
00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:31.710
Okay.

94
00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:40.050
Weirdly, both Brittany and I have had guest appearances on the TV shows Off Center and Mismatch,

95
00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:42.630
but we were not in the same episode.

96
00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:49.030
So, the office was the third project that she and I have both been in,

97
00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.750
but we've never shared a scene and we've never met.

98
00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:53.350
That's crazy.

99
00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:56.530
Our lives have sort of been intersecting.

100
00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.210
Brittany, am I going to meet you one day?

101
00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:01.930
You're on track to finally be in a project together at the same time,

102
00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.050
on the same day, in the same scene.

103
00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:06.490
So, Angela, there are two more guest star waitresses.

104
00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:10.150
These are the waitresses that Michael and Andy bring back to the party.

105
00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:14.270
So, first, there is Amy, who is played by Kat On,

106
00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:19.610
and she's done just a ton of sketch comedy, stand-up, funny or die.

107
00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:25.710
And then there is Michael's girlfriend, Nikki, who is played by Kulop Vilaisok.

108
00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:26.250
Okay.

109
00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.890
I worked with Kulop on Hot Wives of Orlando.

110
00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:30.370
It was the show on Hulu.

111
00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:35.010
It's a really funny spoof of The Real Housewives, and she was great in it.

112
00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:37.910
She's great in a lot of things, but that was a lot of fun to do.

113
00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:39.790
Well, lady, you know I loved that.

114
00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:40.850
I know.

115
00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:43.090
Okay.

116
00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:51.230
Fast fact number three, I got the scoop from Kentopedia on the Benihana location,

117
00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:53.850
because we got a lot of fan questions about it.

118
00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:59.090
I wanted to ask about that, because I was wondering if it's the Benihana's in Encino.

119
00:05:59.290 --> 00:05:59.990
It sure is.

120
00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:03.390
I have been to that Benihana's, and when I was re-watching this,

121
00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:07.750
the way you walk into the restaurant, you know, it's the same.

122
00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:09.790
And I was like, yeah, come on.

123
00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:12.190
That's Benihana's in Encino, California.

124
00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:17.870
Well, lady, we got some fan questions from Ashley Hildreth, Rachel Martineau,

125
00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:27.090
Hannah Holtman, Malia Holbrook, Abigail Hess, Rupinder Gossel, and many others, all asked,

126
00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:30.350
were the restaurant scenes filmed at an actual restaurant?

127
00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:32.610
How long did it have to be shut down?

128
00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:36.290
And is the chef at Benihana an actual Benihana chef?

129
00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:38.110
Or was it an actor?

130
00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:41.130
I'm going to say yes, because he was too good with that knife.

131
00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:42.870
Yes, we will start there.

132
00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:44.210
It was a real chef.

133
00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:53.190
We hired all of the chefs and all of the waitstaff of the Encino Benihana to perform in the episode.

134
00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:57.850
And we also hired 78 background actors to play diners.

135
00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:00.530
We shut it down for a whole day.

136
00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:02.850
We rented out the entire place.

137
00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.850
And it took about 12 hours to shoot those scenes.

138
00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:12.170
Um, this is reminding me that we had a bigger budget, because when in the past could we go in

139
00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:13.770
and be like, we'll take the whole place?

140
00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:18.570
I mean, even Chili's was like an out of business, boarded up.

141
00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:20.010
Yes, that's right.

142
00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:20.910
Restaurant.

143
00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:26.470
But this was like an actually, like, very successful restaurant that was always full of people.

144
00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:28.310
And we closed them down for a day.

145
00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:28.690
Wow.

146
00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:33.150
Well, and I did a deep dive on Benihana.

147
00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:40.110
And I'd like to give you now the history of Benihana, the restaurant.

148
00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:42.350
Well, I didn't see that coming.

149
00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:44.370
So I'm ready.

150
00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:45.870
It's fascinating.

151
00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:48.650
This is from the Benihana website.

152
00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:54.510
And this is the first line from their description of the history of the Benihana restaurant.

153
00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:56.750
I'm going to quote it because it is fantastic.

154
00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:08.570
Quote, like any good story, the history of Benihana begins with the descendant of a samurai warrior and a small Tokyo coffee shop.

155
00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:11.030
Are you intrigued?

156
00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:12.150
I'm riveted.

157
00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:13.270
Okay.

158
00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:14.410
It's amazing.

159
00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:15.730
So this is what happened.

160
00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:20.010
So there's a couple in Tokyo, Yunusuke and Katsu.

161
00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:22.290
They started a coffee shop.

162
00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:32.510
And in order to set themselves apart, Yunusuke would bike over 20 miles to get real sugar to serve with the coffee in his shop.

163
00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:36.870
And this little detail caused the shop to be a huge success.

164
00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:40.310
The name of their coffee shop was Benihana.

165
00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:42.150
All right.

166
00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:50.150
Cut to years later, the couple's son, Rocky, moves to the United States with dreams of opening a restaurant.

167
00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:54.330
He starts by going to night school to study restaurant management.

168
00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:57.890
And during the day, he sold ice cream on the streets of Harlem.

169
00:08:58.270 --> 00:08:59.490
But here's what he did.

170
00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:03.710
He put a little Japanese cocktail umbrella into the ice cream.

171
00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:05.510
And this really set it apart.

172
00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:15.970
And eventually, he earned $10,000, which he used to open his first Benihana restaurant, naming it after his parents' coffee shop in Tokyo.

173
00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:17.310
Well, that was adorable.

174
00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:21.390
Also, I'm a sucker for anything with a tiny umbrella in it.

175
00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:22.410
Give it to me.

176
00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:24.670
Any drink with a tiny umbrella tastes better.

177
00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:25.750
It really does.

178
00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:26.330
Oh, yeah.

179
00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:27.350
I mean, that's a fact.

180
00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:32.730
I might have saved a few of those, like from special occasions, and they're in my kitchen.

181
00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:34.630
I don't doubt it.

182
00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:35.770
But that is very you.

183
00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:36.710
I know.

184
00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:39.930
My husband's like, do we still need these?

185
00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:42.150
I mean, the little stick is broken.

186
00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:43.390
I'm like, no, don't throw it away.

187
00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:45.790
OK, so there's more to this story.

188
00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:51.770
OK, his first shop, which was on West 56th Street, it was exactly like how it is today.

189
00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:57.490
His vision was to have the Tempanyaki grills set up in front of the customers with the chefs doing all the knife work.

190
00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:09.870
But it was it was struggling at first until six months after they opened, the restaurant got a boost when a famous food critic came in and gave the restaurant a fabulous review.

191
00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:11.970
Here's my favorite part of that.

192
00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:18.630
Can I tell you the name of the food critic, this famous food critic like you wanted this person to come to your restaurant?

193
00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:20.170
Are you ready?

194
00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:20.790
Who?

195
00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:23.170
Clementine Paddleford.

196
00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:24.970
Oh, my God.

197
00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.970
She sounds like a made up character in a Mary Poppins movie.

198
00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:32.090
She sounds like an improv game that I used to do when I did improv.

199
00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:32.870
Hey, guys, come on.

200
00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:34.110
We're going to do Clementine Paddleford.

201
00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:34.810
Let's do it.

202
00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:37.310
Right.

203
00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:39.450
I was like, what is happening?

204
00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:41.170
We're in a Disney film.

205
00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:42.010
I want.

206
00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:42.470
All right.

207
00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:43.270
Wait, wait, wait.

208
00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.390
There's just so many ways I want to use that name now.

209
00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:46.450
Like I want it to be a cocktail.

210
00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:48.610
I'll take a Clementine Paddleford, please.

211
00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:49.870
It comes with an umbrella.

212
00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:56.270
And thank you, Clementine, for giving Benihana a rave review because the restaurant became a huge success.

213
00:10:56.270 --> 00:11:02.270
And there are now over 70 Benihana restaurants in the United States, Caribbean and Central and South America.

214
00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:04.190
How about that?

215
00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:05.870
That is a really good one.

216
00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:06.550
And you know what?

217
00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:12.190
If your name is Clementine Paddleford, you are the best food critic in New York City.

218
00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:17.410
I mean, yes, I have a clear picture of this woman and her discerning food eating.

219
00:11:17.930 --> 00:11:18.130
Yeah.

220
00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:20.710
Oh, I need to see what she looks like.

221
00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:26.670
I only imagine her eating with like very tiny cutlery, like a tiny fork in a...

222
00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:27.170
Right?

223
00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:28.130
Mm-hmm.

224
00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:28.730
Mm-hmm.

225
00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:29.910
Why is that?

226
00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:31.730
All right, lady.

227
00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:36.450
Thank you for indulging my deep dive on Benihana and Clementine Paddleford.

228
00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:37.470
Are you kidding?

229
00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:38.450
I loved it.

230
00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:41.490
I need more Clementine Paddleford in my life.

231
00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:42.130
Same.

232
00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:43.290
Same.

233
00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:47.670
But listen, should we move on now to our first interview?

234
00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:48.710
Yes.

235
00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:49.610
Okay, guys.

236
00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:54.530
We had such a fun storyline with Rashida Jones in this episode with the whole dueling Christmas

237
00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:56.850
parties and it was such a blast to shoot.

238
00:11:57.010 --> 00:12:00.090
We couldn't wait to reunite with her and talk all about it.

239
00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:01.750
So let's get to it.

240
00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:03.930
Sam, run the interview with Rashida.

241
00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:07.670
I'm so happy to see your face.

242
00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:08.470
I know.

243
00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:09.070
Me too.

244
00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:10.110
This is so nice.

245
00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:12.250
I don't think the three of us have been...

246
00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:17.070
Well, we're not in the same place, but we have at least been all talking to each other

247
00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:18.870
for like a decade.

248
00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:19.790
Oh.

249
00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:20.250
Yeah.

250
00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:22.190
All in the same one place.

251
00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:22.570
Yeah.

252
00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:24.190
I think you're right.

253
00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:25.230
That makes me a little sad.

254
00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:28.870
Isabel asked me a really random question the other day.

255
00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:31.890
She's like, Mom, who of your friends have you been on a boat with?

256
00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:40.770
And I was like, well, I mean, Rashida and I went on an ocean conservation trip for Oceana.

257
00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:43.110
So we were on a lot of boats.

258
00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:44.470
Wait, a lot?

259
00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:48.610
I mean, we were like on a boat almost every day.

260
00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:49.050
That's true.

261
00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:49.550
For a week.

262
00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:50.010
That's true.

263
00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:50.950
That's true.

264
00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:53.570
And so we were like clocking how many boats.

265
00:12:53.750 --> 00:12:57.210
And I'm like, well, technically maybe same boat, but a boat every day.

266
00:12:57.610 --> 00:13:02.810
That one dive that we took that day where there was like, it was like the 101 of the ocean.

267
00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:05.170
It was like sharks and eels and turtles.

268
00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:06.390
It was just the cooleSt.

269
00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:07.770
It was the cooleSt.

270
00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:09.150
I know.

271
00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:11.190
Oh, that was great.

272
00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:12.450
I miss all of that.

273
00:13:12.610 --> 00:13:12.950
I know.

274
00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:16.830
But yeah, I just thought it was just like one of those questions your kid asks you.

275
00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:20.150
And then she's like, wait, you've never been on a boat with Jenna?

276
00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:21.970
I was like, no, Jenna.

277
00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:23.590
Have you guys never been on a boat?

278
00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:24.730
We've never been on a boat.

279
00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:26.450
We've got some adventures, Jenna.

280
00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:27.170
You got it.

281
00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:28.290
You got to make that happen.

282
00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:32.590
You might have to do like a recording of your podcast on a boat.

283
00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:35.530
Oh, dear Lord, Rashida, look at our setup right now.

284
00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:38.750
Doesn't it sound good to imagine, though?

285
00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:39.490
Yes.

286
00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:40.910
Yes.

287
00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:44.270
One day we'll be boating and podcasting together.

288
00:13:44.570 --> 00:13:45.170
Oh, God.

289
00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:45.810
I love it.

290
00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:48.090
Well, we can't thank you enough for being here.

291
00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:49.490
We're so thrilled to have you.

292
00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:51.010
I'm so happy to be here.

293
00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:56.430
Well, Rashida, one question we always ask our guests is how did you come to be on The Office?

294
00:13:56.430 --> 00:14:00.430
I auditioned many, many times.

295
00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:06.270
I knew people socially before I joined the caSt.

296
00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:09.690
And I went to college with Mike Schur.

297
00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:11.710
We were in a play together freshman year.

298
00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:12.410
Oh, man.

299
00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:14.030
Oh, I was a doozy.

300
00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:16.230
I would love to see what Mike Schur was like in college.

301
00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:17.130
He was the same.

302
00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:18.070
He was the same?

303
00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:18.290
Yeah.

304
00:14:18.370 --> 00:14:19.750
He was like floppy haired.

305
00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:21.090
I mean, he was a little less.

306
00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:28.050
I remember like in our 20s, he got like a fancy shirt for the first time and making fun of him for a really long time.

307
00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:39.850
So he was like he was like Mike without like any any sort of like socialization, you know, and we were in a like really bad farce together.

308
00:14:39.950 --> 00:14:43.590
And yeah, we became fast friends and we took a lot of classes together.

309
00:14:43.590 --> 00:14:48.710
And anyway, so I knew Mike and I came to know about the U.S.

310
00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:52.870
office because he moved from from New York and said, I got a job in L.A.

311
00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:54.990
and I'm going to write the U.S.

312
00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:55.390
office.

313
00:14:55.670 --> 00:15:01.130
And I was really mad at him, as I think a lot of people were, because we were like, how could you do that?

314
00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:01.730
The U.K.

315
00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:03.010
office, my favorite show.

316
00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:04.390
How could you even try?

317
00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:04.970
Yeah.

318
00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:10.390
And then it was in a lot of ways, you know, its own thing and even better and great.

319
00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:14.190
And, you know, but it was a big mountain, I'm sure, for you all to climb.

320
00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:14.710
Oh, yeah.

321
00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:16.830
My first reaction was like, no, no, no, no, no.

322
00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:17.770
It's perfect.

323
00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:18.530
It's perfect.

324
00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:19.070
Right.

325
00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:19.490
Right.

326
00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:25.290
And then, of course, I was like so thrilled, you know, when it was like Greg and such smart people in the writer's room.

327
00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:29.210
You guys had the benefit of playing characters that didn't have a British counterpart.

328
00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:37.990
Like, I was always worried, oh, no, I don't want people to think that I stole my performance from Lucy Davis.

329
00:15:38.330 --> 00:15:38.590
Right.

330
00:15:38.830 --> 00:15:44.050
And so, but I had seen her performance, and I'm sure it seeped in.

331
00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:44.930
How could it not?

332
00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:47.450
And also, the lines were the same in the pilot.

333
00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:48.050
Right.

334
00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:53.910
But you guys had a big part, I think, of getting to influence your characters.

335
00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:55.730
Is that true on your part, Rashida?

336
00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:57.310
Angela's kind of talked about that before.

337
00:15:57.990 --> 00:16:03.070
There was some, remember there was some third point in a triangle that showed up in the UK office.

338
00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:14.850
There was like a love interest for Tim that came up, but a completely different, like, characterization of somebody who, you know, who would be like a threat to that relationship.

339
00:16:15.090 --> 00:16:23.250
To end that other point, just to say that I auditioned many, many, many times, and I remember having a distinct feeling then when I got to the point, Jenna,

340
00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:32.430
where you and I sat in a room and read lines together, and then got to improv a little bit, like, everything just, like, made sense all of a sudden.

341
00:16:32.810 --> 00:16:36.170
You know, like, it all, it was such a cool, like, counterpart.

342
00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:41.690
Like, it made me feel like, oh, I really, like, I feel like I really get this dynamic, you know?

343
00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:46.890
And I feel like you kind of got me the part in a lot of ways.

344
00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:47.870
No way.

345
00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:49.310
Yes, no, you did.

346
00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:54.610
Well, I had that same experience with you in the room, and it's very similar to when they paired me up with John.

347
00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:59.810
I was like, oh, when I'm paired up with this person, I just turn into Pam.

348
00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:05.830
I think I read with two or three different people, and Mike asked me, so what'd you think?

349
00:17:05.930 --> 00:17:06.470
Who'd you like?

350
00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:09.030
And I just went on and on and on and on and on about you.

351
00:17:10.070 --> 00:17:13.730
And I was like, I mean, she was amazing.

352
00:17:14.130 --> 00:17:15.490
I told him all those things.

353
00:17:15.710 --> 00:17:16.990
She made it so easy to be Pam.

354
00:17:17.190 --> 00:17:18.630
And he was like, okay, noted.

355
00:17:19.210 --> 00:17:22.490
But he didn't say, like, oh, she's my best friend from college.

356
00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:23.630
I can't wait to tell her.

357
00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:25.670
So he played it so cool.

358
00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:34.730
No, and by the way, with me, too, like, I wish if there were a time I really wanted a part, and I could, like, curry some favor with people I knew, this was the time.

359
00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:37.710
But, like, nobody would do anything for me.

360
00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:39.230
Because you know how Greg is, too.

361
00:17:39.310 --> 00:17:41.390
I mean, he was, like, asking everybody.

362
00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:42.910
He could not make up his mind.

363
00:17:43.030 --> 00:17:45.770
There was, like, another woman who was kind of trying to decide between us.

364
00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:47.290
I came in so many times.

365
00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:48.550
I waited for so long.

366
00:17:48.910 --> 00:17:50.870
Mike, you know, Mike is, like, ethical Mike.

367
00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:54.390
So he wasn't going to be like, I'm going to just, I'm going to gun for you.

368
00:17:54.390 --> 00:17:57.950
He was like, it has to be the right person for the part, and everybody has to be on board.

369
00:17:58.090 --> 00:18:00.530
So, like, it was torturous.

370
00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:06.090
Like, they really, really took me to the bitter end before I got the call.

371
00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:07.690
I'm so glad you got the part.

372
00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:09.750
It changed my whole career.

373
00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:10.490
Yeah.

374
00:18:10.690 --> 00:18:14.590
Yeah, I was, like, about to quit acting before I got this show.

375
00:18:14.650 --> 00:18:17.070
So it was a big deal for me.

376
00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:22.290
For Jenna and I, this was the first time I had rewatched the whole Benihana Christmas, like, all in one sitting.

377
00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:22.830
Yeah.

378
00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:28.670
I had seen little clips because, you know, it's the little drummer boy, and people, like, send me clips of me doing that.

379
00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:30.870
But I hadn't really sat down and watched it all.

380
00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:32.070
And what about you?

381
00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:33.690
Was this the first time you rewatched it?

382
00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:34.530
Yes.

383
00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:35.850
I hadn't seen it.

384
00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:37.030
I don't think since it aired.

385
00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:40.010
And I was giggling so much.

386
00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:42.850
And, like, a couple things occurred to me.

387
00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:46.170
One was that we got that payoff, Jenna.

388
00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:53.630
Finally, like, that thing that, like, came to life in the audition, I don't think it really got to really manifest until this episode, you know?

389
00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:57.750
Because up to now, we were kind of, like, rivals and, like, sniffing each other's butts.

390
00:18:57.750 --> 00:18:58.570
That sounds gross.

391
00:18:58.710 --> 00:18:59.290
But you know what I mean?

392
00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:01.190
If we were dogs, we would have been doing that.

393
00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:02.030
Yeah.

394
00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:11.570
And this is the first time that, like, I think the humanity in both characters recognized the mutual humanity in the other one and was like, you know what?

395
00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:15.550
But, like, it's Christmas and we work in these closed quarters.

396
00:19:15.590 --> 00:19:19.010
And also, we have a common enemy in Angela.

397
00:19:19.330 --> 00:19:26.730
So, it was, like, it just was easy for us to, like, to be a team, which I thought that was really cool.

398
00:19:26.890 --> 00:19:31.510
I don't mean to toot our own horns, but I think we had amazing chemistry in this episode.

399
00:19:31.750 --> 00:19:33.970
I just, I mean, we are adorable.

400
00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:35.190
Can I say it?

401
00:19:35.290 --> 00:19:36.090
You can say it.

402
00:19:36.210 --> 00:19:36.990
You can say it.

403
00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:38.710
There could have been a spinoff.

404
00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:40.730
That's all I just want to say.

405
00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:41.470
Totally.

406
00:19:41.690 --> 00:19:43.270
Pam and Karen, Christmas special.

407
00:19:43.570 --> 00:19:43.970
Yes.

408
00:19:44.210 --> 00:19:49.770
I can tell you guys, we would hang out between scenes and I would just start cracking up and just be with you guys.

409
00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:51.350
I'd be like, yay, I'm hanging out.

410
00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:52.330
It's the girls.

411
00:19:52.570 --> 00:19:56.870
And then, like, on action, I had to be so mean to both of you.

412
00:19:56.870 --> 00:19:59.490
And then you, like, got to have this whole other hangout.

413
00:19:59.630 --> 00:20:00.190
It was hard.

414
00:20:00.530 --> 00:20:01.210
I know.

415
00:20:01.650 --> 00:20:05.670
Phyllis, Phyllis really gets to emote how much she's, like, missing the other party.

416
00:20:05.670 --> 00:20:07.690
I wasn't able to, but that's how I felt.

417
00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:08.210
I know.

418
00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:08.850
I know.

419
00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:12.530
But it's good because it all plays out in the episode because we, like, we joined forces.

420
00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:12.890
Yeah.

421
00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:13.370
Yeah.

422
00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:18.430
We did what we needed to do, which was just, like, teach you a lesson and help you to stop being so controlling.

423
00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:20.350
And then you got to sing, you know?

424
00:20:20.690 --> 00:20:21.290
Oh, God.

425
00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:21.910
I was terrified.

426
00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:24.730
I don't like singing in front of people, you guys.

427
00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:25.470
That was really hard.

428
00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:26.070
Oh, God.

429
00:20:26.390 --> 00:20:33.350
Listen, I had to do it at that, in that wedding episode where I had to sing The Police, and I was so scared.

430
00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:35.670
Do you remember that episode?

431
00:20:35.970 --> 00:20:36.830
Whose wedding was it?

432
00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:38.030
Is that Phyllis's wedding?

433
00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:39.610
Phyllis's wedding, yes.

434
00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:40.350
Oh, yeah.

435
00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:41.170
Yeah.

436
00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:44.450
It is interesting time to watch these episodes again.

437
00:20:44.710 --> 00:20:46.290
You know, Jenna and I have talked about this a little bit.

438
00:20:46.530 --> 00:20:48.650
That was interesting for me to rewatch it now.

439
00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:49.710
How was it for you?

440
00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:54.330
I mean, listen, that was the second thing I was going to say is watching the off.

441
00:20:54.430 --> 00:20:57.030
I haven't watched the show since it aired.

442
00:20:57.270 --> 00:21:11.750
And, you know, I think so much of the show and so much of Michael and what works about Michael is this delicate balance between full cringe and, like, well-intentioned, lovable,

443
00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:14.590
like, bad boss.

444
00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:20.470
And to watch it now in the current climate, like, that balance feels different.

445
00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:21.690
It just feels so different.

446
00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:22.990
I mean, there's so much stuff.

447
00:21:23.090 --> 00:21:24.510
It's not – it's beyond cringe.

448
00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:26.790
It's, like, wow.

449
00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:35.270
But it's also, to me, like, you know, I think there's so much conversation right now about what's acceptable, but also, like, in retrospect,

450
00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:45.010
what we're allowed to kind of partake in, you know, in a time where things were considered more acceptable, even if they weren't and they shouldn't be.

451
00:21:45.010 --> 00:21:55.390
I think it's good to have things that read really badly now and didn't read badly at the time because it is a mark of our progress.

452
00:21:55.790 --> 00:22:05.190
And it's also, like, the show resonates because Michael reminds people of their bosses and people they work with and maybe a little bit of themselves.

453
00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:11.790
And it's, like, really important that people see that, like, you have to measure up to the moment that you're in.

454
00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:16.590
So much of the show is, like, people putting up with, like, his bad behavior and being, like, oh, my God.

455
00:22:16.770 --> 00:22:21.590
And now I think people feel armed at work to be, like, you know what?

456
00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:26.150
I don't like the way you're talking about that person or that woman or that whatever.

457
00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:28.470
Like, I don't – that's not acceptable behavior anymore.

458
00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:33.370
And you could say something and feel empowered as, like, an employee to do that, you know?

459
00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:35.810
So it was super fascinating.

460
00:22:35.810 --> 00:22:46.410
I think a lot of times we, on the show, as characters, we would speak up by giving a look to camera or indicating our disapproval.

461
00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:47.690
But you're right.

462
00:22:47.870 --> 00:22:51.770
I think today we would speak up right to his face.

463
00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:54.770
Michael wouldn't have a job with that behavior probably.

464
00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:55.370
No!

465
00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:57.910
It's one too many infractions, Michael.

466
00:22:58.090 --> 00:22:58.250
Jeez.

467
00:22:58.550 --> 00:23:00.490
He's like a human resources nightmare.

468
00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:03.370
And that's the funny thing, too, is that Toby is the enemy.

469
00:23:03.370 --> 00:23:08.070
Like, Toby's trying to – like, he's sensitive and he's trying to take care of people and be tolerant.

470
00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:12.610
And, like, Michael hates him so much for it because he's killing his party.

471
00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:18.250
And, like, this feels like a relationship you couldn't even write today, really, you know?

472
00:23:18.410 --> 00:23:23.150
One of the notes that Greg gave Steve when Steve was, like,

473
00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:31.150
starting to perform as Michael is that he saw the character as this guy who thinks maybe Jennifer Aniston might see this tape someday.

474
00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:34.230
Like, so to sort of play it, like –

475
00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:34.390
Right.

476
00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:39.110
And I so see in Steve's performance how Michael Scott thinks he's an entertainer.

477
00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:43.470
He thinks he's funny and cutting edge and pushing boundaries.

478
00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:45.190
And he's just so inappropriate.

479
00:23:45.230 --> 00:23:49.470
But I see his performance in a different way knowing that note from Greg.

480
00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:50.030
Totally.

481
00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:54.870
Well, also because, you know, there's a conversation around who is entitled to push the boundaries.

482
00:23:54.870 --> 00:23:59.990
And I think that entertainers feel like they can in a way that normal people can't.

483
00:24:00.110 --> 00:24:05.490
And so it would make sense that, like, Michael would think he's one of those entertainers and not one of those normal people, you know?

484
00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:06.250
Oh, yeah.

485
00:24:06.410 --> 00:24:06.530
Yeah.

486
