00:58:57.880 --> 00:59:01.640
Many people have made videos on how to wrap a gift with only three pieces of tape,

1221
00:59:01.640 --> 00:59:03.880
so it can be done, but-

1222
00:59:03.880 --> 00:59:04.740
Hold up, lady.

1223
00:59:05.100 --> 00:59:05.400
Yeah.

1224
00:59:05.540 --> 00:59:10.960
People commenting on your tape, that's got to be in your family, because who you're giving

1225
00:59:10.960 --> 00:59:13.600
at Christmas, you're like with your family.

1226
00:59:13.980 --> 00:59:18.080
So you've got like some aunt that's like, oh, you use more than three pieces.

1227
00:59:18.760 --> 00:59:19.100
Yeah.

1228
00:59:19.320 --> 00:59:19.640
Yeah.

1229
00:59:19.640 --> 00:59:20.620
I get grief.

1230
00:59:20.820 --> 00:59:24.480
I get grief from my family and some friends-

1231
00:59:24.480 --> 00:59:25.920
For not being your character.

1232
00:59:26.920 --> 00:59:27.420
Yeah.

1233
00:59:27.560 --> 00:59:30.160
I mean, that's the tricky thing about having a character

1234
00:59:30.160 --> 00:59:33.920
that you played for a long time who makes grand declarations.

1235
00:59:34.020 --> 00:59:36.680
It gets superimposed onto me.

1236
00:59:37.940 --> 00:59:38.940
But, you know.

1237
00:59:39.180 --> 00:59:42.380
Do you know what I get almost every single year from a family member?

1238
00:59:43.240 --> 00:59:43.780
What?

1239
00:59:43.780 --> 00:59:48.220
I get at least one gift that has a cat on it.

1240
00:59:48.900 --> 00:59:49.100
Yeah.

1241
00:59:50.340 --> 00:59:51.700
I like cats, Jenna.

1242
00:59:51.700 --> 00:59:52.900
Because Angela Martin likes cats.

1243
00:59:53.060 --> 00:59:53.300
Yeah.

1244
00:59:53.460 --> 00:59:54.460
I like cats.

1245
00:59:54.600 --> 00:59:59.700
I don't need a gift every year with a cat on it, guys, from my own family.

1246
01:00:00.160 --> 01:00:08.820
My favorite is, more than once, more than once, I have gotten a cat calendar, and there's

1247
01:00:08.820 --> 01:00:15.800
usually a photo of a cat hanging for dear life from a tree, and it says, hang in there.

1248
01:00:16.300 --> 01:00:16.900
Yeah.

1249
01:00:16.900 --> 01:00:19.200
I've gotten that calendar more than once.

1250
01:00:19.280 --> 01:00:20.060
Oh, no.

1251
01:00:23.260 --> 01:00:24.500
Okay, wait, wait, wait.

1252
01:00:24.560 --> 01:00:32.380
We have to get back to the scene because they're flirting, and then Karen sees the flirt, sees Roy, and is like, he's cute.

1253
01:00:32.380 --> 01:00:33.280
You should date him.

1254
01:00:33.620 --> 01:00:37.320
And then Pam does not say a word.

1255
01:00:37.520 --> 01:00:40.360
She doesn't own up, and she just says, maybe.

1256
01:00:40.930 --> 01:00:41.180
Well,

1257
01:00:41.420 --> 01:00:54.600
I feel like Pam can't tell Karen that she used to be with Roy without it opening up a lot of questions that might lead her to having to tell her things that she knows Jim hasn't told her.

1258
01:00:54.780 --> 01:00:55.420
I agree.

1259
01:00:55.660 --> 01:01:01.740
Well, lady, I think we should pause our breakdown for this episode for a moment because this is kind of the halfway mark,

1260
01:01:01.740 --> 01:01:06.200
and we thought that this would be a good place to play our interview with Jen Salata.

1261
01:01:06.200 --> 01:01:11.640
Yeah, I think it would be great to hear from the writer of this episode before we dig any deeper.

1262
01:01:12.200 --> 01:01:14.180
All right, Sam, will you play the interview with Jen?

1263
01:01:15.920 --> 01:01:17.220
Hi, Jen Salata.

1264
01:01:17.540 --> 01:01:18.260
Hi, Jen.

1265
01:01:18.560 --> 01:01:19.420
You guys look gorgeous.

1266
01:01:19.560 --> 01:01:22.560
You look, it literally looks like time stopped since I last saw you.

1267
01:01:22.880 --> 01:01:23.700
I love you.

1268
01:01:23.880 --> 01:01:24.920
You stop it.

1269
01:01:25.020 --> 01:01:25.680
I'm so excited.

1270
01:01:25.680 --> 01:01:36.760
My favorite guests are when we have writers on because you guys have this perspective that is totally different from the actors, and I could just listen to you guys talk forever.

1271
01:01:37.120 --> 01:01:39.440
It's my favorite side of the story.

1272
01:01:40.260 --> 01:01:43.400
That and probably Phil Shea having to make all the weird props.

1273
01:01:44.260 --> 01:01:52.780
All I remember about Phil Shea is constantly, if I had to come in on, like, a Saturday or a Sunday because I needed to do a little work or I forgot something at the office,

1274
01:01:52.900 --> 01:01:54.060
he was always there.

1275
01:01:54.060 --> 01:01:55.540
He was always there making a prop.

1276
01:01:55.720 --> 01:01:57.420
Like, he just never stopped working.

1277
01:01:57.660 --> 01:01:59.940
He was just brilliant, you know?

1278
01:02:00.260 --> 01:02:03.040
Yeah, my memory of him is him running.

1279
01:02:03.260 --> 01:02:07.100
He was always, like, across the place, and he had things in his hand.

1280
01:02:07.100 --> 01:02:12.580
And I'd be like, hey, Phil, I'd be like, hey, I just got to attach this hat to the squirrel, and then I'd have to, I'm like, what?

1281
01:02:13.600 --> 01:02:22.640
What made me laugh when you told me that he, I forgot that he presented everything on a tray, and the bird thing made me laugh so hard because I'm sure that is, I can imagine that,

1282
01:02:22.660 --> 01:02:23.640
I can picture that.

1283
01:02:24.200 --> 01:02:32.740
And I wanted to tell you, when I was listening to the bird stories that you guys were telling from your own home, but I Googled it,

1284
01:02:32.740 --> 01:02:40.100
and I found out that there was an article about why we're seeing dead birds and birds that are struggling now, and it's because of quarantine.

1285
01:02:40.560 --> 01:02:41.960
It was the most interesting article.

1286
01:02:41.960 --> 01:02:49.580
They said that we are just around, and we're noticing nature more, and we're more aware because we're not, like, in our offices all day long,

1287
01:02:49.600 --> 01:02:54.220
so that, unfortunately, this is a time where birds struggle, but we don't usually see it.

1288
01:02:54.480 --> 01:03:00.220
So I don't know if that's, like, yay or boo or somewhere in between, but I thought that was interesting.

1289
01:03:00.220 --> 01:03:08.840
This is the big bird baby boom season this time of the year, and a big part of, because I looked it up too, Jen,

1290
01:03:09.140 --> 01:03:18.660
a big part of a bird's life cycle is that between the nest and being a full adult bird, they actually nest on the ground.

1291
01:03:18.660 --> 01:03:26.220
So the parents kind of kick them out of the tree, and they nest under bushes or on the ground for a couple of days,

1292
01:03:26.220 --> 01:03:30.480
and their parents visit them, but it's sort of like that's like their going-to-college phase.

1293
01:03:30.700 --> 01:03:35.600
You know, they're out of the nest, but their parents are still kind of, like, sending them money from time to time.

1294
01:03:35.820 --> 01:03:45.260
And so in that little period, like, 80% of birds die because they are, you know, peril meets them.

1295
01:03:45.260 --> 01:03:51.660
Maybe it's, you know, a raccoon or a cat or something, a squirrel.

1296
01:03:52.180 --> 01:03:56.060
Yeah, this is the part of their life where they meet the most doom.

1297
01:03:56.880 --> 01:03:58.160
Oh, this is sad.

1298
01:03:58.160 --> 01:04:03.820
But I read that somebody rescued another person, like a bird fell into a pool, but they rescued it.

1299
01:04:03.960 --> 01:04:09.560
So it's like there may be more rescues happening because we're more aware and vigilant right now.

1300
01:04:10.380 --> 01:04:21.200
So cut to next year, an article that says, a rise in birds due to people intervening and saving them has now bled us of all of the gnats.

1301
01:04:21.300 --> 01:04:25.700
And it turns out gnats were necessary, but the birds ate the ball.

1302
01:04:26.120 --> 01:04:29.160
I know this has been any on at Christmas, and I'm sure you will deal with this,

1303
01:04:29.280 --> 01:04:36.200
but, like, I do remember reading articles that people's lives were saved because of staying alive in the safety training.

1304
01:04:36.280 --> 01:04:37.540
Is it safety training?

1305
01:04:37.540 --> 01:04:40.320
In the episode, um, Stress Relief.

1306
01:04:40.500 --> 01:04:41.440
Stress Relief.

1307
01:04:41.640 --> 01:04:44.640
Yeah, because I remember I wrote that.

1308
01:04:44.800 --> 01:04:51.040
I wrote that, and I remember having to make sure, I remember wanting to make sure it was correct in case anybody watched the show and actually used it.

1309
01:04:51.140 --> 01:04:57.600
But then I started to hear news reports that people remember it from the office and are saving people's lives from that.

1310
01:04:58.300 --> 01:04:59.760
I've read those same articles.

1311
01:05:01.900 --> 01:05:02.720
It's crazy.

1312
01:05:03.700 --> 01:05:05.560
You're saving lives, Jen Salata.

1313
01:05:05.640 --> 01:05:05.900
Well.

1314
01:05:07.540 --> 01:05:08.140
I don't know.

1315
01:05:08.820 --> 01:05:14.380
Jen, we always like to ask people when they come on the podcast how they got their job on the office.

1316
01:05:14.620 --> 01:05:19.140
I was on the show Malcolm in the Middle, which I really enjoyed that show.

1317
01:05:19.140 --> 01:05:26.480
But I, every, every morning after the, after the office aired, I would come in and just talk about the office forever.

1318
01:05:26.760 --> 01:05:29.380
And they were like, let's get to work on Malcolm stories.

1319
01:05:29.600 --> 01:05:30.720
And I was like, yeah, yeah, sure.

1320
01:05:30.940 --> 01:05:32.900
Did you see what happened on diversity day?

1321
01:05:32.900 --> 01:05:34.880
So, I was a big fan.

1322
01:05:35.240 --> 01:05:43.780
Um, and I actually, uh, wrote a short story about a woman who falls in love with the middle of a man.

1323
01:05:43.780 --> 01:05:49.420
She works in quality control in a button factory and she sees the middle of people walking by window all day long.

1324
01:05:49.420 --> 01:05:52.940
And she puts all her hopes and dreams in the middle of this man.

1325
01:05:53.240 --> 01:05:54.060
Oh my God.

1326
01:05:57.450 --> 01:05:57.790
It was so brilliant.

1327
01:05:58.390 --> 01:05:59.590
It was so crazy.

1328
01:05:59.810 --> 01:06:01.470
I had never written a short story before.

1329
01:06:01.570 --> 01:06:02.690
I wrote it.

1330
01:06:02.790 --> 01:06:07.450
It took me forever to write it because I took me forever to sit down and figure out what I had to say.

1331
01:06:07.610 --> 01:06:09.130
If any, did I have anything to say?

1332
01:06:09.250 --> 01:06:10.990
And it kind of, anyway, I wrote it.

1333
01:06:11.090 --> 01:06:14.510
And then Suzanne Daniels, I found out that they have piles of scripts.

1334
01:06:14.630 --> 01:06:19.490
The Daniels house has piles of scripts to the point where the kids have told me that they almost forget that they're scripts.

1335
01:06:19.490 --> 01:06:24.170
They think of them as furniture, like a coffee table, somewhere to put their legs up.

1336
01:06:24.510 --> 01:06:32.890
And Suzanne Daniels, I guess, grabbed my short story, probably thinner than the rest of the scripts on the pile, and responded to it and gave it to Greg.

1337
01:06:33.030 --> 01:06:39.010
And then I met him at a coffee bean, had a meeting right before he was about to go to Upfronts for season two.

1338
01:06:39.370 --> 01:06:46.690
And, um, uh, just, uh, probably begged to be on the show, was really excited and just, uh, loved him, loved meeting him.

1339
01:06:46.810 --> 01:06:48.110
And that's how it happened.

1340
01:06:48.110 --> 01:06:51.530
Oh my gosh, that is such a fantastic story.

1341
01:06:52.030 --> 01:06:56.870
Well, Jen, we have a question for you about, you know, obviously we're talking Benny Hanna Christmas today.

1342
01:06:57.330 --> 01:06:59.870
What was the inspiration for this episode?

1343
01:07:00.170 --> 01:07:03.790
Do you remember how it came about, how you came to be the person to write it?

1344
01:07:03.910 --> 01:07:06.890
Like sort of the, the beginning of what was Benny Hanna?

1345
01:07:07.110 --> 01:07:14.050
The initial idea was Michael goes through a breakup and I can almost see myself leaping out of a chair at that moment.

1346
01:07:14.050 --> 01:07:23.710
If that came, when I came from the room saying this, I need to have this episode because I just felt like taking Michael through a breakup is just like having, well,

1347
01:07:23.710 --> 01:07:26.130
how would a 14 year old boy deal with a breakup?

1348
01:07:26.130 --> 01:07:30.710
You know, and just kind of just the riding those waves for the first time.

1349
01:07:30.910 --> 01:07:39.930
Has he ever been that close to a person to have, you know, to a romantic partner to have those waves so you can really take him to the point where he's lying on the floor?

1350
01:07:39.930 --> 01:07:49.210
Or so I put in a little bit of 14 year old boy and then a little bit of me, you know, listening to James Blunt, goodbye, my lover and breakups that I've had.

1351
01:07:50.190 --> 01:07:54.410
So there was a smush of the two of us, my version of Michael's stuff.

1352
01:07:54.410 --> 01:08:06.910
And when I ended up leaving the office, which was devastatingly difficult to do, but I ended up telling Steve that I was leaving and his, I'll never forget his comment to me.

1353
01:08:07.030 --> 01:08:09.590
He said, I think this is going to be really difficult for you.

1354
01:08:09.690 --> 01:08:12.250
And I said, yeah, no, definitely.

1355
01:08:12.550 --> 01:08:16.410
But he's like, because I think you work a lot of your own stuff out through my character.

1356
01:08:17.690 --> 01:08:23.440
It was like, there's this game that people play of like, who are they in the office?

1357
01:08:23.660 --> 01:08:26.460
And I'm like, I can't be Pam or Angela or Phyllis.

1358
01:08:26.620 --> 01:08:27.840
Like, I'm Michael.

1359
01:08:28.180 --> 01:08:29.700
Michael thinks I'm Michael.

1360
01:08:31.840 --> 01:08:33.380
Steve was aware.

1361
01:08:33.660 --> 01:08:36.220
Steve was aware when it was a ginsalata script.

1362
01:08:36.220 --> 01:08:39.280
Oh, well, we're going to work out some issues this week.

1363
01:08:39.600 --> 01:08:40.060
Yes.

1364
01:08:41.420 --> 01:08:48.700
So I, I think that for the breakup, I put myself as being sort of delayed in that area and experiencing a breakup.

1365
01:08:48.840 --> 01:08:54.180
I think my first breakup with a boyfriend was late, you know, mid twenties, you know, just experiencing that.

1366
01:08:54.380 --> 01:08:59.140
And then also just having, you know, just thinking of a young, a younger boy going through it.

1367
01:08:59.240 --> 01:09:01.400
And I just left, I wanted it so badly.

1368
01:09:01.400 --> 01:09:04.300
So I think I probably just wrestled someone to the ground for it.

1369
01:09:04.680 --> 01:09:05.380
Oh my God.

1370
01:09:05.380 --> 01:09:12.500
Well, Jen, I would love to talk to you about the party planning committee versus the committee to plan parties.

1371
01:09:12.780 --> 01:09:13.740
What the hell?

1372
01:09:14.020 --> 01:09:19.940
You were saying on a previous podcast, Jenna, that I really am drawn to Angela and writing for Angela.

1373
01:09:20.240 --> 01:09:24.300
And there was something, I was starting to think about why that was besides just being brilliant.

1374
01:09:24.300 --> 01:09:27.040
And it's so funny and it's just fun to write for you.

1375
01:09:27.040 --> 01:09:34.200
But I was thinking, I can't stand sometimes rules and bureaucracy and all of that stuff.

1376
01:09:34.200 --> 01:09:40.540
And I feel like your character represented so many of those lines and things you can't do and limits.

1377
01:09:40.880 --> 01:09:48.620
So somehow it was like cathartic for me to go deep in there and like, have you push those lines with people.

1378
01:09:48.860 --> 01:09:52.260
You know, comedy writers oftentimes want to push against authority and rules.

1379
01:09:52.260 --> 01:09:54.280
And so you were, you represented that.

1380
01:09:54.500 --> 01:09:56.880
So I just kind of love to give you that.

1381
01:09:56.980 --> 01:09:58.660
So then what do you do to your character?

1382
01:09:59.260 --> 01:10:08.180
You threaten her with a rival party and just kind of up the stakes, a rival planning committee and up the stakes and have you kind of lose it.

1383
01:10:08.500 --> 01:10:14.780
When you run out to try to get the permission from Dwight that the party needs to start now.

1384
01:10:14.780 --> 01:10:24.800
Now it's just like the intensity with which you're clinging to the fact that you need to maintain the balance of your structure is like, I just, I just delayed in that.

1385
01:10:25.160 --> 01:10:26.440
I remember that scene.

1386
01:10:26.580 --> 01:10:34.320
I remember actually talking to you about the dialogue and you and Harold and, and I guess the first take I did it, maybe I didn't have that urgency.

1387
01:10:34.320 --> 01:10:41.140
Like, and, and you guys like pulled me aside and you're like, no, like, like think like the building's on fire.

1388
01:10:41.240 --> 01:10:46.060
Like, you know, and that kind of like stakes.

1389
01:10:46.300 --> 01:10:57.640
And then Jen, while we're talking about this, I had a line where I said to Meredith, as she's deciding which party to go to, I say, Meredith, like, if you go to that party,

1390
01:10:57.720 --> 01:10:59.320
you're going to be very, very sorry.

1391
01:10:59.320 --> 01:11:00.720
I say something like that.

1392
01:11:01.120 --> 01:11:03.000
And then Meredith is like, is that a threat?

1393
01:11:03.380 --> 01:11:06.420
And I go, no, it's an invitation.

1394
01:11:07.600 --> 01:11:11.580
I don't think I really got the joke on the first time I read it.

1395
01:11:11.700 --> 01:11:18.860
And you were like, Ange, were you like, maybe when you say it's an invitation, switch gears to like a smile.

1396
01:11:19.080 --> 01:11:23.000
And I remember it changed the whole scene.

1397
01:11:23.260 --> 01:11:24.880
Like, I love that moment.

1398
01:11:24.880 --> 01:11:32.980
That moment reminds me of in horror movies, when like the deranged ex-boyfriend is like, let me in this house right now.

1399
01:11:33.820 --> 01:11:35.980
And then, then there's like silence.

1400
01:11:36.120 --> 01:11:40.460
And he's like, Monica, I'd love to come inside.

1401
01:11:41.100 --> 01:11:43.660
And you're like, whoa, that is super creepy.

1402
01:11:44.720 --> 01:11:47.160
That's scarier than the banging on the door.

1403
01:11:47.820 --> 01:11:53.300
So that's when I watched that scene, Angela, it's an invitation was like super unnerving.

1404
01:11:53.300 --> 01:11:55.200
It was like, what just happened?

1405
01:11:55.700 --> 01:11:56.760
That was a note.

1406
01:11:56.880 --> 01:11:58.400
That was a note from Jen Salata.

1407
01:11:58.640 --> 01:12:00.060
It's just a peek into her brain.

1408
01:12:00.920 --> 01:12:07.580
Well, I think maybe, but there's definitely an alternate reality where the office is a horror and Angela.

1409
01:12:07.960 --> 01:12:13.740
There are moments where you're standing by the copier and then just, you know, jump out.

1410
01:12:14.860 --> 01:12:25.440
But I feel like there's something where you get to see Angela's struggle of her real feelings in the episode because they leak out all over the place.

1411
01:12:25.560 --> 01:12:27.380
But then the pop back to this is a work.

1412
01:12:27.500 --> 01:12:28.260
This is an office.

1413
01:12:28.540 --> 01:12:29.420
We have to be professional.

1414
01:12:29.820 --> 01:12:37.180
So there's like, we get to see that like moment where you, where you, the threat comes out.

1415
01:12:37.180 --> 01:12:39.800
And then, you know, no, this is office decorum.

1416
01:12:39.920 --> 01:12:41.060
Here I am as well as this person.

1417
01:12:41.060 --> 01:12:44.820
It's so creepy because we just saw what you really are or what part of you is.

1418
01:12:45.120 --> 01:12:45.440
Yeah.

1419
01:12:45.860 --> 01:12:49.840
I love this storyline, too, because it serves that whole purpose.

1420
01:12:49.960 --> 01:12:56.060
And then it also serves the purpose of putting Pam and Karen together and freaking Jim out.

1421
01:12:56.400 --> 01:13:00.220
So that's why I, it's so rich with storyline.

1422
01:13:00.220 --> 01:13:03.380
Should we head over to Benihana?

1423
01:13:03.660 --> 01:13:07.820
Because Jen, we were not there that day that you shot in the restaurant.

1424
01:13:07.820 --> 01:13:10.100
And there are so many delicious scenes.

1425
01:13:10.860 --> 01:13:11.320
Delicious.

1426
01:13:11.700 --> 01:13:15.480
I feel like that was a really good use of, is that like a metaphor?

1427
01:13:15.760 --> 01:13:17.420
Because Benihana serves food?

1428
01:13:17.720 --> 01:13:18.200
I don't know.

1429
01:13:18.260 --> 01:13:19.180
Oh, well.

1430
01:13:19.640 --> 01:13:24.720
I think that the idea of Benihana might have come up because it always cheered me up when I was a kid.

1431
01:13:24.720 --> 01:13:27.900
